        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How We Chose These Treadmills</h2>
<p>
            Not tested hands-on; picked from listings, specs and owner reviews. We did not walk or run on these
            machines. We read each Amazon listing through Amazon’s product API on 24 November 2026, checked the
            maker’s own information where the listing pointed to it, and compared both with what this site has
            already published about each brand. Every figure on the page traces back to one of those sources.
          </p>
          <p>
            Five bars decided the list. <strong>The treadmill had to be on Amazon US under $700 on the day we
            checked</strong>, and we gave preference to machines between $600 and $700, because the cheaper tier
            already has its own guide. <strong>It had to be a full treadmill</strong>, with a console on uprights,
            handrails and a belt at least 18 inches wide, not a walking pad with a fold-up bar. <strong>It had to
            come from a named brand</strong> with a storefront beyond a single listing; we passed over several
            unbranded machines with the same 12 mph and 20% figures, including one sold under the brand name
            “Generic”. <strong>Its basic figures had to be stated</strong>: speed range, incline range and belt size
            at minimum. And <strong>it had to do one job better than the others here</strong>.
          </p>
          <p>
            We also kept clear of the picks on our neighbouring budget pages. The ProForm Carbon TLS, BORGUSI,
            MERACH, Schwinn SFT4.1 and Sunny SF-T7718 on the <a href="/best-treadmills-under-600/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills under $600</a> page, and the NordicTrack T Series machines and Horizon T101 on the under-$800 page, are all reasonable
            buys, but they are covered there. This page is more useful if it shows you the machines that only start
            to make sense once your budget reaches $650 or $700.
          </p>
          <p>
            Because the searches that bring most readers here ask for incline of 12% or more, we gave the most weight
            to the incline range and top speed, then to belt size and weight limit, and then to warranty. We gave
            little weight to peak horsepower and decibel claims, which are the figures budget listings inflate most,
            and none to star ratings.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="incline-and-speed" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Can You Get 12% Incline and 12 mph Under $700?</h2>
<p>
            Yes. Three of the five machines here list both a 12 mph top speed and a maximum incline of 12% or
            more: the Sunny SF-T723016 at 12%, and the Aeriflo X-T510A and ORASANT at 20%. That combination was
            hard to find under $700 even a couple of years ago, and it is still uncommon among established brands
            at this price. Most of the big-name treadmills under $700, including the ProForm and NordicTrack models
            on our other budget pages, stop at 10 mph and 10 or 12% incline.
          </p>
          <h3>What 12% incline means in practice</h3>
          <p>
            A 12% grade rises 12 feet for every 100 feet travelled, which is a steep hill by road standards and
            about the slope most incline-walking routines use as their top setting. Walking at 3 mph on 12% is hard
            work for most people and raises the heart rate far more than the same speed on the flat. Our explainer on <a href="/what-is-12-incline-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">what 12% incline on a treadmill means</a> puts it in degrees and compares it with outdoor hills. If 12% is your target, any of the three 12 mph
            machines gets you there; the Aeriflo and ORASANT give you headroom beyond it.
          </p>
          <h3>Do you need 20%?</h3>
          <p>
            Most people do not. A 20% grade is steeper than almost any road and is closer to a hiking trail. It is
            useful if you are training for mountain hikes, if you find 12% stops being hard, or if you want to keep
            the speed low and the effort high to spare your joints. The catch is that very steep incline at speed asks
            a lot of a budget motor and frame, and both 20% machines here come from online-first brands whose motor
            figures we cannot check independently. If you only ever plan to walk at 10 to 12%, the Sunny is the safer
            buy.
          </p>
          <h3>Do you need 12 mph?</h3>
          <p>
            Twelve miles an hour is a five-minute mile, faster than most recreational runners ever go. What a 12 mph
            ceiling really buys is room for sprint intervals and a motor that is not working at its limit when you
            run at 7 or 8 mph. If you only walk, a 10 mph machine such as the WELLFIT or Schwinn is enough, and the
            money is better spent on deck size, weight limit or warranty.
          </p>
