        <div id="what-700-buys" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What Does $700 Buy That $600 Does Not?</h2>
<p>
            Less than the extra $100 suggests in frame quality, and more than you might expect in features. The
            jump from $600 to $700 rarely buys a heavier deck or a longer warranty from the same brand. What it buys
            on Amazon is speed and incline range: 12 mph instead of 10, and 15 or 20% incline instead of 10 or 12.
            It also widens the choice of 20-inch belts and 55-inch decks, which at $600 are found mainly on the
            better-known brands.
          </p>
          <p>
            Where $700 still falls short is the deck and motor for serious running. The usual guidance for
            regular runners is a 60-inch deck and a continuous motor rating of about 3.0 CHP or more, and none of
            the machines here publishes a continuous rating at all. For that, look at the <a href="/best-treadmills-under-800/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills under $800</a> and the under-$1,000 tier, where 60-inch decks from established brands start to appear.
          </p>
