          <p>
            These ranges are drawn from the listings on our budget pages, not a rule every machine follows. Note the
            odd shape of the middle row: online-first brands at $600 to $700 often list more incline than established
            brands at $800. That is real, but it is a feature figure, not a durability figure, so read it alongside the
            warranty.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="motor-claims" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How Much Should You Trust the Motor Figures?</h2>
<p>
            Treat them as an upper bound. Four of the five listings give a horsepower figure: 3.0 HP on the Sunny
            (labelled peak), 3.5 HP on the Aeriflo, 4.5 HP maximum on the WELLFIT and 5.0 HP on the ORASANT. None is
            labelled as a continuous-duty rating, which is the figure that tells you what a motor can sustain for an
            hour. Schwinn does not give a horsepower figure for the 810 at all and instead backs the motor with a
            10-year warranty. Our <a href="/treadmill-horsepower-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill horsepower guide</a> explains why a 5.0 HP peak figure on a $510 treadmill and a well-made 2.5 CHP motor are not comparable.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two listings, WELLFIT and ORASANT, describe their motors as brushless. A brushless motor has no carbon
            brushes to wear out and is usually a little quieter, which is a genuine advantage, but it says nothing
            about how much power the motor can sustain. Our guide to the <a href="/best-treadmills-with-brushless-motor/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills with brushless motors</a> covers what the label does and does not tell you.
          </p>
          <p>
            For walking and jogging, any motor on this page should be enough. The figures matter more as speed,
            incline and user weight rise together: a 250 lb user jogging at 6 mph on 15% incline works a motor far
            harder than a 150 lb user walking on the flat. If that describes you, give extra weight to the
            weight limit and the motor warranty, which are the two figures that reflect what the maker is prepared
            to stand behind.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="deck-and-weight" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Which Deck Size and Weight Limit Do You Need?</h2>
<p>
            Belt width decides comfort for walkers; belt length decides comfort for joggers. Four of the five
            machines here have a 20-inch belt, which gives room for a natural arm swing and for drifting sideways
            when you tire. The ORASANT is narrower at 18 inches, still enough for most walkers. Length runs from
            51 inches on the ORASANT to 55 inches on the Aeriflo, WELLFIT and Schwinn, with the Sunny in between at
            53 inches. For brisk walking and easy jogging, 51 to 55 inches suits most adults. Taller users and anyone
            planning faster running should favour the 55-inch decks.
          </p>
          <p>
            Incline adds a twist. On a steep grade most people shorten their stride and lean forward, so deck length
            matters a little less, but a long deck still helps if you hold the handrails less and walk naturally. It
            also gives more margin at the back of the belt, which matters more on a slope.
          </p>
          <p>
            For weight limit, the usual advice is to stay about 50 lb under the stated maximum, because a treadmill
            worked near its limit wears faster. That makes the 275 lb Schwinn suitable for users up to about 225 lb,
            the 300 lb Sunny up to about 250 lb, the 400 lb ORASANT up to about 350 lb and the 500 lb WELLFIT the
            choice for the heaviest users. The Aeriflo listing does not state a limit; ask the seller before you order
            if you are over 250 lb. Our <a href="/treadmill-weight-capacity-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill weight capacity guide</a> explains how makers arrive at the figure.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="brand-and-warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Does Brand and Warranty Matter at $700?</h2>
<p>
            Yes, and the warranty is where online-first brands and established brands differ most. Here is what each
            listing says, in the listing’s own terms.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Schwinn 810:</strong> 10 years on the motor and frame, one year mechanical and electrical, one year labour, per the listing. The longest motor cover here.</li>
            <li><strong>Aeriflo X-T510A:</strong> 5 years on the motor, 2 years on the full machine and lifetime support, per the listing. Unusually specific for an online brand.</li>
            <li><strong>Sunny SF-T723016:</strong> the bullets we read do not give a warranty length, but Sunny is a US-based brand with its own store and US support; check the warranty section before you order.</li>
            <li><strong>WELLFIT and ORASANT:</strong> the bullets we read do not state a warranty; ORASANT directs questions to Amazon buyer-seller messaging.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            None of this means the online brands are poor buys. It means the risk sits with you rather than the
            maker once Amazon’s return window closes. If you buy one, buy from a listing sold or fulfilled by Amazon,
            keep the order details, register any warranty on the day it arrives and test every incline level and
            speed in the first fortnight, while returns are simple.
          </p>
          <p>
            One more point about subscriptions. None of the five needs a paid membership to work. Sunny’s SunnyFit
            app and Aeriflo’s Kinomap and FitShow support are optional, and Schwinn’s Explore the World routes need a
            subscription, but each machine runs its speed, incline and built-in programs without one. If avoiding
            fees matters to you, our <a href="/best-treadmills-without-subscriptions/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills without subscriptions</a> guide goes further.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="space-and-noise" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Will a $700 Treadmill Fit and Stay Quiet?</h2>
<p>
            All five are full-size machines, so plan for about 6 feet of floor length in use plus clear space behind
            the belt; owner’s manuals commonly ask for several feet behind the deck so that a fall does not end
            against a wall. Four of the five fold: the Sunny, Aeriflo and ORASANT listings describe hydraulic or
            soft-drop folding, and the Schwinn uses Schwinn’s SoftDrop system. The WELLFIT listing we read does not
            describe folding, so assume it stays flat unless the seller confirms otherwise.
          </p>
          <p>
            Check your ceiling too. On a 20% incline the front of the deck rises several inches, and a jogger lifts a
            few inches with each stride. In a basement with a low ceiling, a tall user on a steep grade can run out of
            headroom on a machine that fits the floor comfortably.
          </p>
          <p>
            On noise, two listings give figures: WELLFIT and ORASANT both claim under 45 dB. We could not check either
            claim, and treadmill noise depends as much on footfall, belt speed and the floor as on the motor. A rubber
            mat under the machine and a ground-floor room do more for the people downstairs than any motor
            specification. If quiet is the priority, our <a href="/quiet-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">quiet treadmills guide</a> covers what actually reduces noise.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="when-to-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Is Black Friday a Good Time to Buy a Treadmill Under $700?</h2>
<p>
            Usually, yes. Treadmill prices in this band move with coupons and lightning deals, and late November and
            January are the periods when they are most often cut. We checked these prices on 24 November 2026, three
            days before Black Friday, so some may already reflect early deals and some may fall further. A machine at
            $750 today can drop under $700 for a day or two, and the reverse happens too.
          </p>
          <p>
            The sensible approach is to choose on specification first and price second. Decide which incline range,
            top speed, deck and weight limit you need, note the price today, and buy when it falls. Our <a href="/black-friday-treadmill-deals-under-1000/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Black Friday treadmill deals under $1,000</a> page tracks the wider tier. A cheap treadmill with the wrong weight limit or deck is not a bargain at any
            discount.
          </p>
          <p>
            Budget for the extras as well: a mat, silicone lubricant for the belt and, for some sellers, a charge for
            delivery beyond the curb. Together they can take a $650 treadmill past $700.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Before You Buy a Treadmill Under $700: 4 Mistakes to Avoid</h2>
          <p>Each of these comes from reading a listing’s headline figures without checking what sits underneath them.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Buying 20% incline you will never use</h3>
              <p>Steep incline is the headline feature at this price, but most incline-walking routines top out around 12 to 15%. If you will never go higher, choose on deck size, weight limit and warranty instead, and treat the extra range as a bonus rather than the reason to buy.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Assuming a missing figure is a good one</h3>
              <p>When a listing leaves out the weight limit, warranty or folding mechanism, do not assume the best. Ask the seller in writing before you order and keep the answer; it is your evidence if the machine does not match.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Reading 5.0 HP as more powerful than 2.5 CHP</h3>
              <p>Peak and continuous ratings measure different things. A budget listing’s 5.0 HP is a short burst figure; a 2.5 CHP rating is what a motor sustains. Where no continuous rating is published, the motor warranty is the better guide.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Forgetting the ceiling on a steep incline</h3>
              <p>A 20% incline lifts the front of the deck several inches, and a jogger rises with every stride. Measure from the floor to the ceiling and allow for deck height, your height, incline lift and bounce, especially in a basement.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">For most homes the best treadmill under $700 is the Sunny Health &amp; Fitness SF-T723016</strong>:
            12 mph, 12% auto incline, a 20 by 53-inch deck and a 300 lb limit for about $646, from a brand with US
            support. If you want steeper hills at running speed, choose the Aeriflo X-T510A, with 20% incline, 12 mph
            and a 55-inch deck; if anyone in the house weighs more than 300 lb, the 500 lb WELLFIT; if the budget is
            closer to $500, the ORASANT, which still reaches 12 mph and 20%; and if you value warranty over speed, the
            Schwinn 810.
          </p>
          <p>
            Whichever you choose, buy on incline range, deck size, weight limit and warranty before horsepower, and ask
            the seller about any figure the listing leaves out. Check the price again before you order, because Black
            Friday is days away. If you need a longer deck for running, the <a href="/best-treadmills-under-800/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">under-$800 picks</a> are the next step up, and if steep walking is the whole point, our <a href="/best-incline-treadmills/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">best incline treadmills</a> guide covers the machines built for it.
          </p>
        </div>

