          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>What is the best treadmill under $700?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For most homes, the Sunny Health & Fitness SF-T723016: 0.6 to 12 mph, 12% auto incline, a 20 by 53-inch deck and a 300 lb limit, for about $646 on Amazon on 24 November 2026. If you want steeper incline, the Aeriflo X-T510A reaches 20% at 12 mph on a 55-inch deck for about $627.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Which treadmill under $700 has 12% or higher incline?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              All five picks except the Schwinn 810 reach 12% or more, per their listings. The Sunny SF-T723016 tops out at 12%, the WELLFIT heavy-duty model at 15%, and the Aeriflo X-T510A and ORASANT 18-inch model at 20%. Of those, the Sunny, Aeriflo and ORASANT also reach 12 mph.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can I get a 12 mph treadmill under $700?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Yes. Three on this page list 12 mph: the Sunny SF-T723016 (about $646), the Aeriflo X-T510A (about $627) and the ORASANT 18-inch model (about $510). Most established-brand treadmills under $700 stop at 10 mph, so a 12 mph machine at this price usually means Sunny or an online-first brand.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is the best treadmill under $700 for heavy people?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              The WELLFIT 15% heavy-duty treadmill, which its listing rates at 500 lb, with a 20 by 55-inch belt, for about $700. The ORASANT model is rated at 400 lb but has a smaller 18 by 51-inch belt. Leave a margin of around 50 lb below any stated limit.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a $700 treadmill good enough for running?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For jogging and intervals, yes; for regular high-mileage running, it is a stretch. The decks here are 51 to 55 inches long and none publishes a continuous motor rating. Runners generally do better with a 60-inch deck and a continuous rating of about 3.0 CHP, which starts closer to $1,000.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is it worth spending $700 instead of $600 on a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              If you want 12 mph and incline above 12%, usually yes, because that combination is much easier to find above $600. If you only walk on gentle grades, the extra $100 buys little you will use, and the under-$600 picks are the better value.
            </p>
